\section{What is read}{

The directives \verb{*CHARACTER LIST}, \verb{*ITEM DESCRIPTIONS},
\verb{*CHARACTER TYPES}, \verb{*NUMBERS OF STATES}, \verb{*NUMBER OF CHARACTERS},
\verb{*IMPLICIT VALUES} and \verb{*DEPENDENT CHARACTERS}, wherever in the given
files they are (so a dataset kept in one file, or split differently, reads
too); other directives are skipped. Character types are unordered
multistate (\code{UM}, read as \code{"categorical"}), ordered multistate (\code{OM},
\code{"ordered"}), integer (\code{IN}, \code{"integer"}), real (\code{RN}, \code{"real"}) and text
(\code{TE}, \code{"text"}).

Attributes follow the DELTA standard: \verb{12,1/2} (state 1 or 2) and \verb{12,1&2}
(1 and 2) both give \code{"state 1/state 2"}, so the difference between "or"
and "and" is not kept; \verb{13,2-3} is a range, which for
an ordered character includes the states in between; \verb{14,U} is unknown
(\code{NA}), \verb{15,-} not applicable (\code{inapplicable}), \verb{16,V} variable (all
states). Numeric values are kept as written, e.g. \code{"7-8.5"} or
\code{"(1-)2-3(-4)"}, which is the numeric notation of the matrix convention.
For a text character the cell is the text of the comment (\verb{7<possibly two species>}). Implicit values are filled in. A dependent character that is
not coded is "not applicable" when its controlling character only takes
the controlling states; the rule itself is kept in the \code{InapplicableIf}
column of \code{characters}. Variant items (\verb{#+}) take the attributes they
leave out from their main item, as the standard says.

Comments (text in angle brackets) are dropped from the names of
characters, states and taxa unless \code{comments = TRUE}, and from the
attributes of non-text characters; the comment of a taxon name (usually
the authority) is kept in \code{taxa$Comment}. A name that consists of nothing
but a comment (\verb{#4. <longevity of plants>/}) is that comment. Typesetting
marks (the RTF control words DELTA programs use, such as \verb{\\i\{\}}, \verb{\\b0\{\}}
or \verb{\\par\{\}}) are removed, and those that stand for a character
(\verb{\\endash\{\}}, \verb{\\ldblquote\{\}}) become that character. Files that are not
valid UTF-8 are read as Windows-1252. The obsolete line sequence numbers
are not supported.

The reader was checked against the datasets distributed with Open DELTA
(among them the "Grass Genera" sample, a flora of 506 taxa and a virus
database of 969 taxa) by comparing every coded attribute with a second,
independent parse of the files.
}

\section{What is written}{

\code{specs} gets the numbers of characters, states and items, the character
types, the numbers of states and the dependent characters; \code{chars} the
character list; \code{items} one item per taxon. Every coded cell is written
out (no implicit values are used), \code{NA} cells are left out, which DELTA
reads as unknown, and cells equal to \code{inapplicable} are written as \code{-}.
The states of a variable taxon are joined with \code{/} ("or"). A numeric cell
that is not a DELTA number or range, and all cells of text characters,
are written as comments. Encoding is UTF-8, and lines are wrapped at 78
columns wherever they have a blank to break at.

A few things cannot be written as they are, because DELTA would read
them as syntax: angle brackets in a name make a comment (which
\code{readDELTA()} drops unless \code{comments = TRUE}); a star or, in a name, a
numero after a blank is put in parentheses (\verb{(*)}, \verb{(#)}); the blank
after a slash in a name is dropped. No file written by dimoose has been
read into a DELTA program (Confor, Intkey, Open DELTA).
}

